    topic: "Teenage sleep research",
    passage: "Dr Malika Rahimova has spent eleven years studying why teenagers in Samarkand sleep so badly, and her latest findings, published last spring, have surprised even her colleagues. Her team monitored one hundred and fifty students aged fifteen to seventeen for six weeks. Contrary to what most parents assume, the students who slept least were not those who spent the most hours on their phones, but those who had the most irregular bedtimes. Going to bed at ten on Monday and at half past one on Saturday, she explained, confuses the body clock far more than a consistently late night does.\n\nThe study also challenged a popular belief about weekends. Many teenagers claim that sleeping until noon on Sunday repays the debt they built up during the week. Rahimova is sceptical. 'You can recover some alertness,' she says, 'but memory remains impaired for several days.' Students who tried to catch up at weekends performed no better in Monday tests than those who slept only six hours throughout, although they reported feeling more cheerful, which she believes is rather misleading.\n\nThe most practical result concerned light. Half of the participants were asked to avoid bright screens for the last hour before bed, while the other half carried on as usual. After three weeks, the first group fell asleep on average twenty minutes earlier, but they did not sleep longer overall, because most of them still woke at the same early hour for school. Rahimova therefore argues that the real obstacle is not the students' habits but the school timetable, which begins at a quarter to eight. She is campaigning for lessons to start at nine, a change that has worked in several European cities, though she admits it would be costly and unpopular with bus companies.\n\nCritics point out that the study had limits. All participants came from the same city, and none of them worked part-time, so the findings may not apply to students who finish evening jobs at eleven. Rahimova accepts this and has already begun a second project involving three hundred students from rural districts. Until then, her advice is modest: keep bedtime within an hour of the same time every night, dim the lights early, and do not rely on weekend lie-ins. Several school directors have promised to read the report, but none has yet agreed to change the timetable.",
