    topic: "A gym membership",
    passage: "In September I signed a twelve-month contract at a new gym called PowerZone, mainly because the salesman was charming and the first month cost almost nothing. He promised me a personal programme, free towels and 'cancellation whenever you like'. I paid forty dollars a month, which seemed reasonable for a place with a swimming pool. For the first three weeks I went four times a week, posted photos online and felt magnificent. By November, I was going once a week, and by January I had not entered the building for six weeks.\n\nWhat changed? Partly it was the crowds. Between six and eight in the evening, the machines are occupied, and people queue with towels as if for a bus. Partly it was the weather, since the walk from my flat takes twenty minutes, and nobody enjoys it in the snow. But if I am honest, the main problem was motivation. I had bought the membership the way some people buy exercise books at the start of the school year: as a promise to a better version of myself, who, unfortunately, never turned up to use it.\n\nWhen I finally decided to cancel in February, I discovered that the salesman's promise had been somewhat creative. The contract required written notice at least thirty days in advance, and a fee of eighty dollars if I left before the end of the year. At the reception, a polite girl apologised and said she could not change the rules. I wrote to the manager, who replied that I could freeze the membership for up to two months for free. That seemed pointless, since I had no desire to return, but a friend, a lawyer, advised me to be patient and keep every email.\n\nIn the end, the matter was solved without a fight. After my friend's letter mentioned the consumer protection law, the manager reduced the fee to forty dollars and cancelled the contract from the end of March. I lost money, but I also learned something. Now I run in the park behind my building, which costs nothing, and I never sign anything before reading the final page. The salesman, incidentally, has left PowerZone; I hope it was for reasons unrelated to me. Anyone considering a gym, I would say: try a month-to-month plan first, and be honest about what kind of person you really are.",
