    topic: "Stress before exams",
    passage: "Every June, the corridors of the National University in Tashkent change character. The cheerful noise of the spring term gives way to whispered conversations, pale faces and an unusual amount of energy drinks. Counsellor Shirin Abdullayeva, who has worked at the university's advice centre for eight years, says that the number of students seeking help roughly triples during the exam period. 'Most of them are not failing,' she observes. 'In fact, many of the most anxious students are the ones with the best marks, because they set impossibly high standards for themselves.'\n\nAbdullayeva identifies three habits that make exam stress worse. The first is studying through the night. Although it feels heroic, memory consolidates during sleep, so a student who revises until four and sleeps for three hours often remembers less than someone who stops at midnight. The second habit is comparing oneself with others. Social media is full of classmates announcing that they have 'already finished the whole syllabus', and it is rarely true. The third, perhaps the most damaging, is catastrophic thinking: the conviction that one poor result will ruin one's entire future.\n\nThe centre offers a surprisingly simple programme. Students learn to divide their revision into forty-minute blocks separated by ten-minute breaks, and to write down worries on paper rather than carrying them in their heads. There are also group sessions in which students describe their fears aloud. One participant, Jahongir, a third-year economics student, says that hearing others admit they felt equally lost was the biggest relief. 'I thought I was the only one who understood nothing,' he laughs. 'It turned out the whole room was pretending.' He now studies in the library with a timer and, to his surprise, scored higher in his statistics exam than the previous year.\n\nNot everyone is convinced. Professor Anvarov, who teaches law, argues that some pressure is both normal and useful, and that universities risk raising a generation unable to cope with ordinary difficulty. Abdullayeva agrees up to a point. 'A little tension sharpens attention,' she says. 'The problem begins when it prevents sleep, appetite or concentration.' Her advice to parents is equally direct: ask your children how they are, not what marks they expect. The first question helps; the second often adds to the weight they already carry.",
