    topic: "A village doctor",
    passage: "For thirty-one years, Dr Kamola Sodiqova has been the only doctor in the mountain village of Sarvak, a place that disappears from the road for four months each winter when the snow closes the pass. Her clinic consists of two rooms, a refrigerator for vaccines and, as she likes to remind visitors, a bicycle that she has not ridden since 2010 because the paths have become too steep for her knees. Instead, she borrows a neighbour's donkey whenever a house call is needed, a creature she describes as 'more reliable than most ambulances and considerably more stubborn than most patients'.\n\nHer working day rarely resembles a city doctor's. At seven she opens the clinic, where perhaps a dozen people are already waiting, some of whom have walked for two hours through mud or dust, depending on the season. By ten she has usually delivered advice rather than medicine, because half of her patients suffer from problems that tablets cannot cure: back pain from carrying water, coughs from smoky stoves, loneliness after children have moved to the capital. 'I have noticed that three of my patients out of ten want to talk more than they want treatment,' she says. 'Listening is part of the prescription.'\n\nEquipment is the great difficulty. The village has electricity only from six to eleven in the evening, which limits what she can do with the small ultrasound machine donated by a charity. Seriously ill patients must travel ninety kilometres to the district hospital, a journey that takes three hours in summer and is sometimes impossible in January. Two years ago, a boy with appendicitis was saved only because a shepherd carried him down the mountain on his back. Since then, the villagers have collected money for a second-hand jeep, although they still need a driver willing to take the risk.\n\nYounger doctors often ask Sodiqova why she never moved to a city. She answers without hesitation. 'In a city, I would be one doctor among two thousand. Here, I know every child's birthday and every grandfather's temper.' She admits that she is tired and has begun training a nurse, Zarnigor, to take over in the long term. But when asked whether she will retire soon, she shrugs. 'Perhaps when the road is repaired,' she says, 'which, as people here say, will happen the same year the donkey learns to read.'",
