    topic: "The sugar tax",
    passage: "When the government announced a sugar tax in the spring of 2021, soft-drink companies predicted disaster, health campaigners predicted miracles, and most ordinary shoppers simply shrugged. Two years later, the results are more complicated than either side promised. The tax, which adds the equivalent of fifteen cents to every litre of drink containing more than five grams of sugar per hundred millilitres, has raised about forty million dollars. Officials had hoped that much of the money would be spent on school meals and sports facilities, and, to be fair, roughly two thirds of it has been.\n\nThe most visible change, however, has not been in what people buy but in what companies sell. Faced with the tax, several manufacturers reduced the sugar in their recipes to just below the limit, so that their products escaped the charge altogether. A popular orange drink now contains nearly a third less sugar than before, and most customers have not noticed. Public-health economist Dr Laylo Mirzaeva calls this 'the quiet victory', since it improves diets without asking anyone to change habits. She estimates that the average child now consumes about six teaspoons less sugar a week than in 2020.\n\nCritics, however, remain unconvinced. The Consumers' Union argues that the tax is unfair because poorer families spend a larger share of their income on cheap fizzy drinks and therefore pay proportionally more. Some shopkeepers near the border report that customers drive across to buy cola abroad, while others say that people have simply turned to fruit juice, which is often just as sweet and is not taxed at all. Dr Mirzaeva admits that this loophole is a genuine weakness, but she notes that extending the tax to juice would be politically difficult, since parents consider juice a healthy product.\n\nWhether obesity rates will fall is still unknown, since it takes years to see such changes. For now, the evidence suggests that the tax has been neither a failure nor a triumph: a modest success, perhaps, in a field where modest success is rare. What seems clear is that the strongest effect came not from punishing consumers but from pressuring producers. Dr Mirzaeva's own conclusion is cautious. 'Taxes alone cannot teach people to eat well,' she says. 'But they can persuade companies to stop making it so difficult.'",
