    topic: "Healthy ageing",
    passage: "At eighty-two, Halima Qodirova swims a kilometre every Tuesday and Friday at the municipal pool in Urgench, and she is irritated when people call her 'remarkable'. 'I am not remarkable,' she says, towelling her hair. 'I am merely stubborn.' She began swimming only seven years ago, after her doctor warned her that her blood pressure and bones were deteriorating. Until then, she had not entered the water since childhood, and she was so nervous on the first day that she held the pool's edge for ten minutes before letting go.\n\nGeriatric specialist Dr Sanjar Ibragimov, who has studied older residents of the region for two decades, says that Halima is a good example, though not an unusual one. His research suggests that the strongest predictor of healthy ageing is not diet, wealth or even genes, but regular movement combined with social contact. 'We found that people who walk daily and have at least three close friends live, on average, four years longer than those who are physically active but isolated,' he explains. 'Loneliness appears to be as harmful as smoking, which surprises almost everyone.' He is quick to add that the findings show a link, not a proof of cause.\n\nHalima's own routine confirms this. After her swim, she meets four friends in the pool cafe for tea, and the conversation, she admits, is more tiring than the exercise. They discuss grandchildren, politics and the shocking price of apricots. She eats simply, with plenty of vegetables and little red meat, but refuses to give up sweets entirely. 'A life without halva is not a life,' she declares. Dr Ibragimov does not object. He says that rigid rules often fail among older people, who tend to abandon any plan that makes them miserable.\n\nThere are, however, obstacles. Many older residents cannot afford pool tickets, and public transport in the evening is unreliable. The city offers discounts for pensioners on Tuesday mornings, but only a handful of people use them, partly because they do not know about the scheme. Halima has therefore taken matters into her own hands: she posts notices in the market and walks neighbours to the pool herself. 'Nobody should wait until eighty to discover they like water,' she says. 'I wasted seventy-five years, but I intend to enjoy the rest.'",
