    topic: "A cooking class",
    passage: "I signed up for the Wednesday cooking class at the community centre in Chilonzor mainly because my doctor had suggested that I eat less oil, and I assumed that a course called 'Light Kitchen' would be full of retired women exchanging recipes. I was half right. Of the twelve participants, nine were indeed women over fifty, but there were also two university students and a taxi driver named Sherzod, who confessed that he had eaten in cars for eleven years and wanted to stop. Our teacher, a former hotel chef named Ravshan Oripov, welcomed us in a spotless apron and immediately banned the phrase 'I can't cook'.\n\n'Nobody is born able to cook,' he said, 'but everybody is born able to taste.' The first lesson, therefore, had nothing to do with recipes. He gave us five bowls of almost identical tomato soup and asked us to say what was missing from each. The answers were salt, acid, sweetness, fat and heat, though I confidently guessed only two. The point, he explained, was that most people add salt whenever a dish tastes flat, when a squeeze of lemon or a pinch of cumin would often fix the problem without the health risks. I wrote this in my notebook and underlined it twice.\n\nOver the following weeks we cooked a surprising range of dishes: baked fish with herbs, a carrot and chickpea stew, and a version of plov made with brown rice and only half the usual oil. The plov caused the loudest debate. Two of the older women declared it an insult to tradition, while Sherzod, who had been sceptical, asked for a second plate. Oripov refused to take sides. 'My job is not to defend plov or attack it,' he said, 'but to show you that you have choices.' By the fourth week, even the doubters had agreed that the dish, though different, was not bad at all.\n\nOn the final evening each of us cooked a three-course dinner for the others, and I made the mistake of choosing a dessert that required exact timing. It collapsed, naturally. Nobody minded; we ate it with spoons and called it a pudding. What I value most from the course is not any single recipe, but a change in attitude. I now read labels, taste before I season and, most importantly, enjoy standing in the kitchen. My doctor says my cholesterol has dropped slightly, although I suspect the greater improvement is in my mood. Sherzod, I should add, now brings his lunch to work.",
