    topic: "Screen time for children",
    passage: "At the parents' evening of School Number Twelve in Andijan, the usual complaints about homework and canteen food were set aside for a more urgent subject: children and screens. The head teacher, Mavjuda Nazarova, opened the meeting with a survey of her pupils' habits. Among children aged eight to eleven, the average daily screen time outside lessons had reached three and a half hours, which was an hour more than two years earlier. 'I am not here to blame anybody,' she said. 'I am here because teachers can see the effect every morning, in tired eyes and short tempers.'\n\nThe guest speaker, paediatrician Dr Otabek Raximov, tried to calm the room. He explained that research does not show screens to be uniformly harmful. A child who spends an hour video-calling a grandmother abroad or building a game with a friend is in a different situation from one who scrolls silently through short videos until midnight. 'The question is not only how long, but what and when,' he said. The strongest evidence, in his view, concerns sleep: children who use devices in the hour before bed take longer to fall asleep and wake up more often. He recommended a simple rule: no screens in bedrooms after eight.\n\nSeveral parents objected. Dildora, a nurse who works night shifts, said that such rules are easy for people with regular jobs, whereas she depends on tablets to keep her son occupied while she sleeps in the daytime. Another father argued that banning devices would make his children look strange among classmates, who all use messaging groups for homework. Dr Raximov listened patiently and conceded that perfect solutions do not exist. He suggested that families should aim for 'good enough': one screen-free meal a day, outdoor play at weekends and, above all, adults who put their own phones down. 'Children copy what we do, not what we say,' he remarked, 'and at this moment half the audience is checking messages.' There was laughter, followed by several embarrassed hands retreating into pockets.\n\nThe meeting ended with a modest decision rather than a dramatic ban. The school will hold a screen-free reading hour each Thursday afternoon, and a working group of five parents will meet monthly to suggest further ideas. Nazarova admitted that the plan was small, but she hoped it would start a conversation inside homes as well as inside the school. 'If children see that we take the question seriously,' she said, 'they are more likely to take it seriously too.'",
