    topic: "A first-aid course",
    passage: "My company sent all of its employees on a compulsory first-aid course last month, and I attended with the reluctance of a man who expects an afternoon of slides. I could not have been more wrong. The instructor, a paramedic named Ulugbek who looked far too young to have seen so much, began by asking us to describe the worst emergency we had ever witnessed. The stories were sobering. One colleague had watched a stranger collapse in a bus queue while everyone stood still, each waiting for somebody else to act. 'That,' said Ulugbek, 'is the real enemy: not ignorance, but hesitation.'\n\nHe then taught us in the order that mattered. First, check that the scene is safe, because a second casualty helps no one. Second, call the emergency number, and preferably send another person to do so while you start helping. Third, begin the actual treatment. For an adult who is not breathing, this means pressing hard in the centre of the chest about one hundred times a minute, which is close to the rhythm of a well-known pop song. We practised on plastic dummies for forty minutes, and my arms were trembling by the end. Ulugbek warned that real chest compressions often break ribs, and that this is acceptable. 'A broken rib heals,' he said. 'A stopped heart does not.'\n\nThe second half of the course dealt with more ordinary accidents, and here our beliefs were corrected repeatedly. Burns should be cooled with running water for at least twenty minutes, not covered in butter or toothpaste, as several of us had learned from our grandmothers. Nosebleeds are treated by leaning forward, not backward, so that the blood does not run down the throat. And a person who is choking but can still cough should be encouraged to keep coughing; hitting their back is only for the case when they cannot make any sound at all. I was astonished how many of my confident assumptions were wrong.\n\nAt the end, each of us received a certificate valid for three years, though Ulugbek told us frankly that the paper matters less than the confidence. He urged us to repeat the practice every six months, because skills fade faster than people expect. I left tired but oddly cheerful. On the way home, I caught myself looking around the metro carriage, wondering who would act first if somebody fell. I decided, uncomfortably, that it might have to be me. Our manager has already announced that next year the course will be offered to our families, which I think is the best decision the company has made in a decade.",
